.TH KAGI-RUN 1 "November 2026" "Kagi 0.39.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kagi-run \- launch a command with Linux scheduling and CPU affinity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kagi-run
.RB [ \-\-policy " " \fIPOLICY\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-priority " " \fIN\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-cpus " " \fILIST\fR ]
.B \-\-
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Applies and verifies scheduling and CPU affinity before replacing itself
with the requested command.
Worker threads and child processes inherit these settings unless they
change them.
The launcher passes arguments directly to the executable.
.PP
Scheduling or affinity errors prevent the command from starting.
Real-time policies require appropriate operating-system permission, such
as a sufficient
.B RLIMIT_RTPRIO
or
.BR CAP_SYS_NICE .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-policy " normal|round-robin|fifo"
Choose normal, round-robin, or FIFO scheduling.
The default is
.BR normal .
.TP
.BI \-\-priority " N"
Use zero for normal scheduling or 1 through 99 for a real-time policy.
The default is zero.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpus " LIST"
Use the given comma-separated logical CPU numbers from the process's
allowed CPU set.
A real-time policy must leave at least one allowed CPU outside the selected
set.
If omitted, the launcher uses all allowed CPUs for normal scheduling and
excludes the first allowed CPU for a real-time policy.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the Kagi version.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
kagi-run \-\-policy normal \-\-cpus 2,3 \-\- kagi-config \-\-wizard
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kagi-config (1),
.BR sched (7),
.BR capabilities (7)
